[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Compressed uploads (flightmd_core/services/compressed.py)

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-013
STATUS: Active
TITLE: A plain file comes back unchanged
STATEMENT: >>>
`open_log(path, max_bytes)` reads the first 4 bytes. When they start with neither `\x1f\x8b` (gzip) nor
`PK\x03\x04` or `PK\x05\x06` (zip) it returns `(path, cleanup)` with the same path and a cleanup that does
nothing, and creates no directory.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: intake
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-004

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-014
STATUS: Active
TITLE: An archive is unpacked into a private directory
STATEMENT: >>>
For a gzip or zip file `open_log` creates a directory with `tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="flightmd-")`, unpacks the
log into it as `log` plus the inner name's extension (lower case; dropped unless it is a dot and 1 to 8 letters
or digits, `_extension`), and returns that path and a cleanup that removes the directory and ignores errors. On
any exception during unpacking or the content check it runs the cleanup and re-raises.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: intake
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-004
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-005

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-015
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Nested archive found by content
STATEMENT: >>>
After unpacking, `open_log` raises `ValueError` ("The archive contains another archive; ...") when the first 8
bytes of the unpacked file start with a gzip, zip, bzip2 (`BZh`), xz, 7z or rar (`Rar!`) signature.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: intake
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-005

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-016
STATUS: Active
TITLE: gzip source
STATEMENT: >>>
`_gzip_source(path)` returns a function that opens the file with `gzip.open(path, "rb")` and, as the inner
name, the base name of `path` with a trailing `.gz` (case ignored) removed.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: intake
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-004

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-017
STATUS: Active
TITLE: zip entries: unsafe paths, clutter, exactly one file
STATEMENT: >>>
`_zip_source` raises `ValueError` "The zip file is damaged: ..." when `zipfile.ZipFile` raises `BadZipFile`.
For every entry, with backslashes turned into slashes, it raises `ValueError` "The zip has an entry with an
unsafe path (...)" when `_unsafe_name` is true: the name starts with `/`, starts with a letter and a colon, or
has a `..` component. It then ignores directories and names matching `__MACOSX/`, `.DS_Store`, `Thumbs.db` or
`desktop.ini` (case ignored) and raises `ValueError` "A zip upload must contain exactly one log file; this one
has N." unless exactly one entry is left.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: intake
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-004
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-005

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-018
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Password protected zip member
STATEMENT: >>>
`_zip_source` raises `ValueError` "The zip is password protected." when bit 0 of the member's general purpose
flags is set.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Inspection
VERIFICATION_NOTE: >>>
No test builds an encrypted zip (the standard library cannot write one). A review checks the test
`info.flag_bits & 0x1` and that it comes before the member is opened.
<<<
ALLOCATION: intake
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-005

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-019
STATUS: Active
TITLE: zip member: declared size, declared ratio, archive extension
STATEMENT: >>>
`_zip_source` raises `ValueError` when the member's declared size is above `max_bytes` (message with both in
MB of 1 000 000 bytes), when the declared size is above `RATIO_FLOOR_BYTES` = 1 048 576 and above `MAX_RATIO` =
200 times the declared compressed size (taken as at least 1) ("The zip expands far more than a flight log can;
refusing it."), and when the member's extension is one of `.zip .gz .tgz .tar .7z .rar .bz2 .xz` ("The zip
contains another archive; ..."). Otherwise it returns the inner base name and a function that reopens the zip
and gives the member stream.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: intake
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-005
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-006

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-020
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Member stream closes its zip
STATEMENT: >>>
`_ClosingMember` wraps the open member and its `ZipFile`; `read(n)` reads from the member and leaving the
`with` block closes the member and then the `ZipFile`.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Inspection
VERIFICATION_NOTE: >>>
No test names this requirement; the behaviour is exercised only indirectly by other tests. A review reads
`_ClosingMember` and checks that closing the stream closes the zip.
<<<
ALLOCATION: intake
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-004

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-021
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Capped copy
STATEMENT: >>>
`_copy_capped(opener, out, max_bytes, packed_size)` copies in chunks of `CHUNK` = 65 536 bytes and stops before
writing the chunk that takes the total above `limit = min(max_bytes, max(200 * packed_size, 1 048 576))`,
where `packed_size` is the size of the archive file. It then raises `ValueError` "The decompressed log is over
the N MB limit." when the total is above `max_bytes`, else "The archive expands far more than a flight log can;
refusing it.".
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: intake
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-006

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-022
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Damaged archive data
STATEMENT: >>>
`_copy_capped` turns `gzip.BadGzipFile`, `EOFError`, `zlib.error` and `zipfile.BadZipFile` raised while opening
or reading into `ValueError` "The archive is damaged or incomplete: ...". As built no other exception is
converted; a zip member with a compression method the runtime cannot read raises `NotImplementedError`.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test, Inspection
VERIFICATION_NOTE: >>>
A truncated gzip is tested. The unconverted `NotImplementedError` is found by review of the `except` clause.
<<<
ALLOCATION: intake
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-005

