[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Row buffer (flightmd_core/services/columns.py)

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-023
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Construction
STATEMENT: >>>
`ColumnBuffer(columns, size=0)` keeps the column names as a list, raises `ValueError` when two names are equal,
and allocates one float64 block of `max(int(size), MIN_ROWS)` rows (`MIN_ROWS` = 256) by the number of columns.
`len()` of a new buffer is 0.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: intake
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-024

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-024
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Appending a row
STATEMENT: >>>
`ColumnBuffer.append(row)` stores the row as float64 at index `n` and adds 1 to `n`; `None` is stored as NaN and
a value NumPy cannot convert to a float raises `ValueError`. When the block is full it first doubles the number
of rows, keeping the stored rows; a block sized exactly by `size` is not reallocated while `n` stays within it.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: intake
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-024

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-025
STATUS: Active
TITLE: The table
STATEMENT: >>>
`ColumnBuffer.frame()` returns a `pandas.DataFrame` holding a copy of the first `n` rows under the column
names, all float64; later changes to the buffer do not change it, and an empty buffer gives an empty frame with
the columns.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: intake
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-024

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Parse cache (flightmd_core/cache.py)

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-026
STATUS: Active
TITLE: When the cache is on
STATEMENT: >>>
`enabled()` reads `FLIGHTMD_CACHE` trimmed and lower-cased: when it is not empty the cache is on unless the
value is `0`, `false`, `no` or `off`; when it is empty or unset the cache is on exactly when
`FLIGHTMD_DATA_DIR` is set and not empty.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: intake
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-009

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-027
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Entry path and key check
STATEMENT: >>>
`cache_dir()` is `<FLIGHTMD_DATA_DIR>/cache`, or `data/cache` under the working directory when the variable is
unset or empty. `_path(sha256)` raises `ValueError` unless the key matches `^[0-9a-f]{64}$` and returns
`<cache_dir>/<sha256>.v2.pkl.gz`, where 2 is `CACHE_VERSION`. `get` and `put` return before this check when the
cache is off.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: intake
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-009

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-028
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Reading an entry
STATEMENT: >>>
`get(sha256)` returns `None` when the cache is off or the entry file does not exist. When the file's
modification time is more than `FLIGHTMD_CACHE_TTL_S` seconds old (default 3600) it deletes the file and returns
`None`. Otherwise it loads the gzip pickle, sets the file's modification time to now (errors ignored), and
returns `(topics, params, metadata)` with the metadata rebuilt by `FlightMetadata.model_validate`. As built the
time limit therefore runs from the last read or write, not from the parse.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: intake
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-009
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-011

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-029
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Unreadable entry
STATEMENT: >>>
`get` logs a warning, deletes the entry (`_drop`, which ignores `OSError` from the delete) and returns `None`
when opening the entry, loading it or rebuilding `(topics, params, metadata)` from it raises `OSError`,
`EOFError`, `zlib.error`, `pickle.UnpicklingError`, `AttributeError`, `ImportError`, `KeyError`, `TypeError` or
`ValueError` (which includes a metadata validation error). The next analysis of the log parses it again.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: intake
COMMENT: >>>
Problem report PR-003, fixed: before, only the first five exceptions were handled and a damaged entry blocked
the log until it expired.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-010

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-030
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Writing an entry
STATEMENT: >>>
`put(sha256, topics, params, metadata)` does nothing when the cache is off. Otherwise it creates the cache
directory, pickles `{"topics", "params", "metadata": metadata.model_dump()}` with the highest pickle protocol
through gzip level 3 into a file `.tmp-*.pkl.gz` in the same directory, renames it onto the entry path with
`os.replace`, and calls `prune()`. On any exception it deletes the temporary file and re-raises.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: intake
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-009
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-010
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-011

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-031
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Pruning
STATEMENT: >>>
`prune()` does nothing when the cache directory does not exist. It deletes every `.tmp-` file older than 3600 s
and every other `*.pkl.gz` file that is older than `FLIGHTMD_CACHE_TTL_S` (default 3600 s) or does not end in
`.v2.pkl.gz`. It then deletes the remaining entries oldest first, by modification time, until their total size
is at most `FLIGHTMD_CACHE_MAX_MB` (default 1024) times 1 000 000 bytes. Files that vanish meanwhile are
skipped.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: intake
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-009
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-010

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-032
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Numeric settings
STATEMENT: >>>
`_env_float(name, default)` in `cache.py` and in `orchestrator.py` returns `float()` of the environment
variable, or of the default when it is unset, and returns the default when `float()` raises `ValueError`. As
built the texts `nan` and `inf` are accepted as values.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test, Inspection
VERIFICATION_NOTE: >>>
Tests set valid numbers for the time limit, the size cap, the log size limit and the parse timeout. No test
sets text that is not a number; a review checks the `except ValueError`.
<<<
ALLOCATION: intake
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-027

