[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-035
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Calling the parser
STATEMENT: >>>
`_parse(log_format, path, report)` makes a new instance of `PARSERS[log_format]` and returns
`parser.parse(path)`, passing `progress=report` only when a callback was given and the parser's `parse` has a
parameter named `progress`.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: intake
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-008
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-014

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-036
STATUS: Active
TITLE: The parser process
STATEMENT: >>>
`_child(log_format, path, q)` runs `_parse` and puts `("done", result)` on the queue. It puts
`("progress", frac)` only when `frac` is at least 0.01 above the last one sent. On any exception, including
`BaseException`, it puts `("error", e)`, replacing an exception that cannot be pickled by
`RuntimeError("<type>: <text>")`.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test, Inspection
VERIFICATION_NOTE: >>>
Every sample-log test with the default timeout takes the result through this function. No test makes a parser
raise inside the child; a review checks the error branch.
<<<
ALLOCATION: intake
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-008

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-037
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Parse with a time limit
STATEMENT: >>>
`_parse_with_limit(log_format, path, timeout_s, report)` calls `_parse` in the calling thread when `timeout_s`
is 0 or less. Otherwise it starts `_child` as a daemon process of the `spawn` context, waits on the queue in
steps of at most 1.0 s, passes each progress value to `report`, re-raises an `"error"` value and returns a
`"done"` value. When `timeout_s` has passed on the monotonic clock it raises `ValueError` "Parsing the log took
longer than N s and was stopped.".
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: intake
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-008

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-038
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Dead parser process and clean-up
STATEMENT: >>>
When the queue is empty and the process is no longer alive, `_parse_with_limit` waits 1.0 s more for a queued
message and then raises `ValueError` "The parser process stopped unexpectedly (exit code N).". On every way
out it terminates the process if it is alive, joins it for at most 5 s and closes the queue.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Inspection
VERIFICATION_NOTE: >>>
No test kills the parser process. A review checks the `queue.Empty` branch and the `finally` block.
<<<
ALLOCATION: intake
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-008

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-039
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Open, detect, parse
STATEMENT: >>>
`_open_and_parse` runs `open_log(log_path, max_bytes)`, `detect_format` and `_parse_with_limit` in the default
thread pool, with the timeout from `FLIGHTMD_PARSE_TIMEOUT_S` (default 600 s). It re-raises
`UnsupportedFormatError` as `ValueError` with the same text, passes a `ValueError` from the parse on unchanged,
and turns any other `Exception` from the parse into `ValueError` "Failed to parse <format> file: <text>". It
always calls the cleanup of `open_log`, then sets `metadata.source_format` to the detected identifier and
returns `(topics, params, metadata)`. As built an exception from `open_log` that is not a `ValueError` is not
converted.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: intake
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-012
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-017
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-002

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-INT-040
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Parse progress and time left
STATEMENT: >>>
Before parsing, `_open_and_parse` reports 5 % with a first estimate of size in MB divided by
`PARSE_MB_PER_S` for the format (12 ULog, 5 binary dataflash, 2 text dataflash, 3 telemetry log, else
`DEFAULT_MB_PER_S` = 4) plus the analysis estimate. For each parser fraction `frac` it reports
`5 + int(55 * min(frac, 1.0))` % with the message "Parsing flight log" followed by an ellipsis and the percentage,
and time left `spent * (1 - frac) / frac` plus the analysis estimate when `frac` is above 0.02, else the first
estimate less the time spent, never below 0. These calls are scheduled onto the event loop from the parse
thread and are not awaited.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: intake
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-INT-014

